"Boudewijn Waijers" <bwaijers@tiscali.REMOVETHIS.nl> writes:

> I don't think access rights to the directories / and /home are of any
> importance. They may be unreadable and unaccessible; only access rights to
> /home/hawk count.

They don't count only if any path you might use to access files under
/home/hawk is relative to /home/hawk (and doesn't start with "../").  If
you specify a path like "/home/hawk/filename" then /, /home, and
/home/hawk must be searchable for "filename" to be accessible.

The only reason some things might work even if / or /home are
inaccessible is that typically a user gets dumped into his home
directory by "login" while it's running as root and can access the
directories that the user cannot; once there, the user can access any
files under his home directory by relative pathnames but any use of
absolute pathnames will fail if any part of the path is not accessible
to the user.

